upPermissionEvaluator.java", "line": 117, "severity": "low", "category": "doc_defect", "title": "Javadoc on canManageMembership contradicts V2 semantics", "body": "The interface Javadoc for `canManageMembership(GroupModel)` says it returns true if the caller has `MANAGE_USERS` \"Or if it has a permission to `MANAGE` the group or `MANAGE_MEMBERSHIP` of the group.\" However `GroupPermissionsV2.canManageMembership` actually also delegates to `hasPermission(..., MANAGE_MEMBERSHIP)`, which corresponds to the documented behaviour (MANAGE or MANAGE_MEMBERSHIP). The V1 implementation, by contrast, accepts MANAGE_MEMBERSHIP only (via `hasPermission(group, MANAGE_MEMBERSHIP_SCOPE)`) and treats a `canManage(group)` match (which itself requires MANAGE on the specific group, not just MANAGE on all-groups) as a shortcut. The single Javadoc comment cannot describe both contracts accurately. Either split the behaviour or document that the V2 implementation effectively treats `MANAGE` of the group (anywhere) as sufficient to manage membership of that group, which is a broader grant than V1."}, {"file": "services/src/main/java/org/keycloak/services/resources/admin/permissions/GroupPermissionEvaluator.java", "line": 123, "severity": "low", "category": "doc_defect", "title": "Javadoc on canViewMembers contradicts V2 semantics", "body": "The Javadoc for `canViewMembers(GroupModel)` describes the V1 contract (which was just `VIEW_MEMBERS`). Both the V1 (post-PR) and V2 implementations additionally accept `MANAGE_MEMBERS`. Either the comment should be updated to mention `MANAGE_MEMBERS` as well, or the implementations should only accept `VIEW_MEMBERS`. Note that accepting `MANAGE_MEMBERS` is a behaviour change relative to what the V1 implementation did before this PR (it used to return `hasPermission(group, VIEW_MEMBERS_SCOPE)` only), and the Javadoc does not warn administrators that a `manage-members` grant now implicitly grants view-members."}]}
